
# The device's traits (abstract native module, native-dom-0012): what kind of device a view is drawn on, read from the
# platform and watched for change. The build rewrites `native/{platform}/device` to the env's impl: the browser reads
# media queries, the toolkit reads UITraitCollection, NSAppearance and Android's Configuration, and the memory host
# answers fixed values a test can set. See note/term/app/11-uniform-interface.md, "two axes of customization".
#
# A trait and its values, each decided by the platform's own notion of it:
#
#   idiom          phone | tablet | desktop     what the platform calls the device (UIDevice idiom; Android's
#                                               smallest width at 600dp; the web's coarse pointer and narrow viewport)
#   width-class    compact | regular            the width the view has now, which changes in split view and on rotation
#   pointer        touch | fine                 the primary input
#   color-scheme   light | dark                 the appearance the person chose
#   reduce-motion  yes | no                     whether they asked for less motion

# the trait's value now, one of the values above
task read-trait
  take name, like text
  like text

# run `handler` with a trait's name and new value whenever the platform says it changed. Answers a remover
task watch-traits
  take handler
    like task
      take name, like text
      take value, like text
  like task
